content='**What was wrong?**  \nIn the original snippet the `|` was used after a field name (e.g. `name: |`), but it was not followed by a properly‑indented block. In YAML a `|` (or `>`) is only allowed when you’re going to put a multi‑line string underneath it.  \nBecause the block didn’t exist, the parser threw *“found a stray \'|\'”*.\n\n**How to fix it**  \nSimply remove the stray `|` (or `>`), keep the indentation correct, and let Helm interpolate the values.  \nBelow is a minimal, working example that you can drop into a Helm chart.\n\n---\n\n## 1.  Directory layout\n\n```\nmychart/\n├─ Chart.yaml\n├─ values.yaml\n└─ templates/\n   └─ deployment.yaml\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2.  `Chart.yaml`\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: v2\nname: mychart\ndescription: A Helm chart for Kubernetes\ntype: application\nversion: 0.1.0\nappVersion: "1.0"\n```\n\n---\n\n## 3.  `values.yaml`\n\n```yaml\n# Deployment details\ndeployment:\n  name: myapp\n  namespace: default\n  replicas: 2\n\n# Container details\ncontainer:\n  name: myapp\n  image: myrepo/myapp\n  imageTag: "1.0.0"\n  imagePullPolicy: IfNotPresent\n  port: 8080\n\n# Secret that holds all the keys\nsecrets:\n  name: myapp-secrets\n\n# Resource limits/requests\nresources:\n  memoryLimit: "512Mi"\n  cpuLimit: "500m"\n  memoryRequest: "256Mi"\n  cpuRequest: "250m"\n```\n\n---\n\n## 4.  `templates/deployment.yaml`\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: Deployment\nmetadata:\n  name: {{ .Values.deployment.name }}\n  namespace: {{ .Values.deployment.namespace }}\nspec:\n  replicas: {{ .Values.deployment.replicas }}\n  selector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app: {{ .Values.deployment.name }}\n  template:\n    metadata:\n      labels:\n        app: {{ .Values.deployment.name }}\n    spec:\n      containers:\n        - name: {{ .Values.container.name }}\n          image: "{{ .Values.container.image }}:{{ .Values.container.imageTag }}"\n          ports:\n            - containerPort: {{ .Values.container.port }}\n          # Pull all keys from the secret into the environment\n          envFrom:\n            - secretRef:\n                name: {{ .Values.secrets.name }}\n          # If you want to reference keys individually (e.g. for validation)\n          env:\n            - name: APP_ENV\n              valueFrom:\n                secretKeyRef:\n                  name: {{ .Values.secrets.name }}\n                  key: APP_ENV\n            - name: APP_PORT\n              valueFrom:\n                secretKeyRef:\n                  name: {{ .Values.secrets.name }}\n                  key: APP_PORT\n            - name: APP_DB_NAME\n              valueFrom:\n                secretKeyRef:\n                  name: {{ .Values.secrets.name }}\n                  key: APP_DB_NAME\n            - name: APP_DB_HOST\n              valueFrom:\n                secretKeyRef:\n                  name: {{ .Values.secrets.name }}\n                  key: APP_DB_HOST\n            - name: APP_DB_USER\n              valueFrom:\n                secretKeyRef:\n                  name: {{ .Values.secrets.name }}\n                  key: APP_DB_USER\n            - name: APP_DB_PASS\n              valueFrom:\n                secretKeyRef:\n                  name: {{ .Values.secrets.name }}\n                  key: APP_DB_PASS\n          resources:\n            limits:\n              memory: {{ .Values.resources.memoryLimit }}\n              cpu: {{ .Values.resources.cpuLimit }}\n            requests:\n              memory: {{ .Values.resources.memoryRequest }}\n              cpu: {{ .Values.resources.cpuRequest }}\n          imagePullPolicy: {{ .Values.container.imagePullPolicy }}\n```\n\n---\n\n### Why this works\n\n* The `|` (pipe) is no longer present – the values are simply interpolated.\n* Indentation is correct: every nested block starts two spaces deeper than its parent.\n* The `envFrom` section pulls **all** key/value pairs from the secret into the container’s environment.  \n  The explicit `env` entries are optional; they’re useful if you need to reference a specific key in a custom way.\n* All variables (`.Values.*`) are defined in `values.yaml`, so Helm can substitute them.\n\n**Result**  \nRunning `helm install my-release ./mychart` will create a Deployment that uses the secret values as environment variables without any YAML parsing errors.' additional_kwargs={} response_metadata={'model': 'gpt-oss:20b', 'created_at': '2025-10-14T04:39:28.484402041Z', 'done': True, 'done_reason': 'stop', 'total_duration': 278000005877, 'load_duration': 181734923, 'prompt_eval_count': 1609, 'prompt_eval_duration': 1420104465, 'eval_count': 25609, 'eval_duration': 276395820498, 'model_name': 'gpt-oss:20b'} id='run--cb8f1ade-5813-4727-bbb0-1251d79c3a0a-0' usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 1609, 'output_tokens': 25609, 'total_tokens': 27218}
